linked_issues.body:
### Description
A BON8 string has no length and ends at the first byte that cannot continue it. When a string is the last thing in a value and is not terminated by `0xFF`, that ending byte (or two bytes, for an integer such as `C2 41`) belongs to whatever follows the value. The reader takes it from the input, keeps it in its private push-back buffer, and finishes the value. With `strict = true` the end-of-input check reads the buffer and reports trailing data. With `strict = false` the buffer is simply discarded, so the next read from the same stream starts behind the lost bytes, without any error.

The documentation promises two things that this contradicts:

- [BON8](https://json.nlohmann.me/features/binary_formats/bon8/): "Input that ends prematurely, trailing data after the value, and a missing final 0xFF after a string at the end of the message are reported as parse_error.110." and "a string at the very end of a message must be terminated by 0xFF."
- [Parsing large and partial input](https://json.nlohmann.me/features/parsing/large_and_partial_input/): "`from_cbor` and its relatives accept `strict = false` to stop after the first value and leave the rest of the stream untouched".

With `strict = false`, the input `61 90` (the string `"a"` directly followed by the integer `0`) is accepted as `"a"`, and the following `from_bon8` call on the same stream finds nothing. With `61 C2 41 90`, the two-byte integer `C2 41` is lost and the next call silently returns `0`, a wrong value rather than an error.

Where the bytes are taken: [`include/nlohmann/detail/input/binary_reader.hpp#L4398-L4419`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/5bad6c518bf6663b69f505511a19c98c91f77c3a/include/nlohmann/detail/input/binary_reader.hpp#L4398-L4419)

```cpp
            // a byte that cannot begin a character ends the string and begins
            // the next value
            if (byte < 0xC2 || byte > 0xF7)
            {
                unget_bon8(byte);
                return true;
            }
            ...
            if (!is_bon8_continuation(second))
            {
                unget_bon8(second);
                unget_bon8(byte);
                return true;
            }
```

Where they are dropped: [`include/nlohmann/detail/input/binary_reader.hpp#L180-L200`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/5bad6c518bf6663b69f505511a19c98c91f77c3a/include/nlohmann/detail/input/binary_reader.hpp#L180-L200). Only the `strict` branch looks at `bon8_pushback`; with `strict = false`, `sax_parse` returns and the reader (and its buffer) is destroyed.

```cpp
        // strict mode: next byte must be EOF
        if (result && strict)
        {
            ...
            else if (input_format == input_format_t::bon8)
            {
                // a string that ends a container hands back the byte after it
                get_bon8();
            }
```

The same happens for a string that is the last element of a counted container (`81 61 90`, that is `["a"]` followed by `0`).

Canonical input is not affected: `to_bon8` always writes the final `0xFF`, and every other place that calls `unget_bon8` reads the byte again right away. The bytes stay in the buffer at the end of a value only when the value ends with an unterminated string, which the BON8 page already declares invalid.

**Since when:** BON8 support is new on develop (#2998); 3.12.0 has no `from_bon8`.
**Security:** Not a vulnerability: no out-of-bounds access, but a receiver that reads several values from one stream with `strict = false` can silently lose or misread the value after a non-canonical one.
**Related:** #2998 (BON8 support), #5677 (input that ends after a UTF-8 lead byte). No open issue or PR found for this. #6053 touches the UTF-8 scan in `simd_scan.hpp`, not this code.

**Possible fix:** In `binary_reader::sax_parse`, before the strict check, report `parse_error.110` when the BON8 value is complete but the push-back buffer is not empty and `strict` is false. With `strict = true` nothing changes (the existing check already reports "expected end of input" on the same byte).

```cpp
        // BON8: a string ends at the first byte that cannot continue it. If
        // that byte follows the value, the reader has already taken it from
        // the input and holds it in bon8_pushback. Without the end-of-input
        // check below it would be dropped, and the next read would start
        // behind it, so report the missing 0xFF instead.
        if (result && !strict && input_format == input_format_t::bon8 && bon8_pushback_size != 0)
        {
            get_bon8();
            return last_byte_error(exception_id::unexpected_end_of_input, "string at the end of the value is not terminated by 0xFF", "value", "");
        }

        // strict mode: next byte must be EOF
        if (result && strict)
```

`get_bon8()` makes the reported position and "last byte" the first byte that does not belong to the value, matching the strict message for the same input.

Options considered:

1. **Report `parse_error.110` (recommended).** Matches `bon8.md`, needs no change in the input adapters, and works for all input types. It changes behaviour for `strict = false` on non-canonical input only, also for pointer, iterator and container input, where the position is not observable and the value used to be returned. Two existing assertions in `tests/src/unit-bon8.cpp` (lines 613 and 617, section "errors / strict mode") check that `{0x81, 'a', 0x90}` and `{0x81, 'a', 0xC2, 0x00}` are accepted with `strict = false`; they must change to expect the exception. BON8 is not in a release yet, so this is not a break against 3.12.0 and needs no opt-in macro.
2. **Give the bytes back to the input.** Keeps the current results and makes the stream position right. But `std::streambuf::sputbackc` and `std::ungetc` guarantee only one byte of push-back, the `C2 41` case needs two, and the bulk window of the stream and `FILE*` adapters would also need to rewind. Much more code for input that the format calls invalid.
3. **Document it.** Leaves the silent loss of a following value; not recommended.

Tested on a copy: yes. With the patch above applied to a copy of `include/`, the reproducer prints the output under "Expected" below, and `tests/src/unit-bon8.cpp` passes except for the two assertions at lines 613 and 617 named above (908,421 of 908,423 assertions pass; Apple clang 21, `-std=c++11 -O1`). With those two assertions replaced by the test below, the whole file passes on the patched copy (908,428 of 908,428).

**Test:** in `tests/src/unit-bon8.cpp`, section `errors` / `strict mode`, replace the two `strict = false` checks of `vec2` and `vec3` and add a stream case:

```cpp
            // the byte after a string that ends a container
            const bytes vec2 = {0x81, 'a', 0x90};
            CHECK_THROWS_WITH_AS(_ = json::from_bon8(vec2, false), "[json.exception.parse_error.110] parse error at byte 3: syntax error while parsing BON8 value: string at the end of the value is not terminated by 0xFF; last byte: 0x90", json::parse_error&);
            CHECK(json::from_bon8(vec2, false, false).is_discarded());
            CHECK_THROWS_WITH_AS(_ = json::from_bon8(vec2), "[json.exception.parse_error.110] parse error at byte 3: syntax error while parsing BON8 value: expected end of input; last byte: 0x90", json::parse_error&);

            const bytes vec3 = {0x81, 'a', 0xC2, 0x00};
            CHECK_THROWS_WITH_AS(_ = json::from_bon8(vec3, false), "[json.exception.parse_error.110] parse error at byte 3: syntax error while parsing BON8 value: string at the end of the value is not terminated by 0xFF; last byte: 0xC2", json::parse_error&);
            CHECK_THROWS_WITH_AS(_ = json::from_bon8(vec3), "[json.exception.parse_error.110] parse error at byte 3: syntax error while parsing BON8 value: expected end of input; last byte: 0xC2", json::parse_error&);

            // with a terminated string, strict = false leaves the next value in the stream
            std::istringstream terminated(std::string("\x61\xFF\x90", 3));
            CHECK(json::from_bon8(terminated, false) == "a");
            CHECK(terminated.tellg() == 2);
            CHECK(json::from_bon8(terminated, false) == 0);

            // without the terminator, the next value would be lost
            std::istringstream unterminated(std::string("\x61\x90", 2));
            CHECK_THROWS_WITH_AS(_ = json::from_bon8(unterminated, false), "[json.exception.parse_error.110] parse error at byte 2: syntax error while parsing BON8 value: string at the end of the value is not terminated by 0xFF; last byte: 0x90", json::parse_error&);
```

`<sstream>` is already included by `unit-bon8.cpp`. The new message must also go into the exception list of `docs/mkdocs/docs/api/basic_json/from_bon8.md` ("Throws parse_error.110 if ... "), for example: "or a string at the end of the value is not terminated by 0xFF".

### Reproduction steps
1. Save the code below as `bon8_strict.cpp`.
2. `clang++ -std=c++17 -O2 -I include bon8_strict.cpp -o bon8_strict && ./bon8_strict`

### Expected vs. actual results
- **Expected:** with `strict = false`, either an error for the unterminated string (`parse_error.110`, as `bon8.md` says), or the value with the following bytes still in the stream. With the proposed fix:
  ```
  61 90   : [json.exception.parse_error.110] parse error at byte 2: syntax error while parsing BON8 value: string at the end of the value is not terminated by 0xFF; last byte: 0x90
  61 FF 90: first="a" tellg=2 second=0
  61 C2 41 90: [json.exception.parse_error.110] parse error at byte 2: syntax error while parsing BON8 value: string at the end of the value is not terminated by 0xFF; last byte: 0xC2
  81 61 90: [json.exception.parse_error.110] parse error at byte 3: syntax error while parsing BON8 value: string at the end of the value is not terminated by 0xFF; last byte: 0x90
  ```
- **Actual:** the first value is returned, and the bytes that follow it are gone from the stream: the second read finds nothing (`61 90`, `81 61 90`) or returns `0` instead of the lost integer `C2 41` (`61 C2 41 90`).

### Minimal code example
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
using nlohmann::json;

static void run(const char* label, const std::string& bytes)
{
    std::istringstream in(bytes);
    try
    {
        const json first = json::from_bon8(in, false);
        std::cout << label << ": first=" << first << " tellg=" << in.tellg();
        const json second = json::from_bon8(in, false, false);
        std::cout << " second=" << (second.is_discarded() ? std::string("<discarded>") : second.dump()) << '\n';
    }
    catch (const json::exception& e)
    {
        std::cout << label << ": " << e.what() << '\n';
    }
}

int main()
{
    // "a" (0x61) followed directly by the integer 0 (0x90): no 0xFF after the string
    run("61 90   ", std::string("\x61\x90", 2));
    // the same with the terminator
    run("61 FF 90", std::string("\x61\xFF\x90", 3));
    // "a" followed by the two-byte integer C2 41: both bytes are lost
    run("61 C2 41 90", std::string("\x61\xC2\x41\x90", 4));
    // ["a"] (0x81 = array of one element) followed by 0x90
    run("81 61 90", std::string("\x81\x61\x90", 3));
    // strict = true on the first input
    try { (void)json::from_bon8(std::string("\x61\x90", 2)); }
    catch (const json::exception& e) { std::cout << "strict, 61 90: " << e.what() << '\n'; }
    try { (void)json::from_bon8(std::string("\x61", 1)); }
    catch (const json::exception& e) { std::cout << "strict, 61: " << e.what() << '\n'; }
}
```

### Error messages
```
61 90   : first="a" tellg=2 second=<discarded>
61 FF 90: first="a" tellg=2 second=0
61 C2 41 90: first="a" tellg=3 second=0
81 61 90: first=["a"] tellg=3 second=<discarded>
strict, 61 90: [json.exception.parse_error.110] parse error at byte 2: syntax error while parsing BON8 value: expected end of input; last byte: 0x90
strict, 61: [json.exception.parse_error.110] parse error at byte 2: syntax error while parsing BON8 string: unexpected end of input
```

### Compiler and operating system
Apple clang 21.0.0 (clang-2100.4.1.1), macOS 27.0.1, arm64

### Library version
develop (5bad6c518b); 3.12.0: n/a (no BON8 support)

